# Palestine v2 — copy to .env and fill in. chmod 600. Never commit.
#
# Rewritten 2026-09-25 from what the code actually reads. Until then this file
# documented eight variables nothing read (an LLM_* block from a T3 adjudicator
# that was never built, and four V1_* paths no code consulted — the v1 tree is
# hard-coded, see PALESTINE_V1_ROOT below) and left out the ones that decide
# whether fires, alarms and backups work at all: a box filled from it ran the
# fires feed as a silent no-op and posted every alarm to an ntfy it did not run.
#
# TWO KINDS OF VARIABLE, AND THE DIFFERENCE MATTERS. No systemd unit loads this
# file as an EnvironmentFile. The first part is read FROM THIS FILE by the code
# itself (resolve/db.py, ingest/telegram_poller.py, ingest/setup_session.py,
# ingest/sources/fires.py, ops/notify.py, ops/backup.py, db/migrate.sh,
# docker/compose.yml via --env-file). The second part is read from the PROCESS
# ENVIRONMENT only: writing it here changes nothing for a running unit — set it
# in a systemd drop-in (`Environment=NAME=value`) or export it in the shell.

# ── read from this file ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PGHOST=127.0.0.1
PGPORT=5433
# db/migrate.sh refuses any other database name.
PGDATABASE=palestine_v2
PGUSER=palestine
PGPASSWORD=
# docker/compose.yml's memory limit for the DB container (default 1536m).
PG_MEM_LIMIT=1536m

# The wb-valhalla container's address ON THE DOCKER NETWORK. Deliberately not
# 127.0.0.1:8002: that host port is bound by honcho-api-1, an unrelated
# application, so pointing here sends every routing request to a stranger who
# answers 404 and every corridor query becomes a 503. Production survived a
# wrong value only because the systemd unit sets no VALHALLA_URL at all and
# falls through to the correct default in code — verify with
# `curl -s $VALHALLA_URL/status | head -c 80`, which must name valhalla.
# ops/sync-valhalla-ip.sh (every 15 min) rewrites this line when the container's
# IP changes. Code default: http://wb-valhalla:8002.
VALHALLA_URL=http://172.22.0.2:8002

# ── Telegram (v2's OWN account — must be a DIFFERENT phone from v1's) ────────
# api_id/api_hash come from my.telegram.org → API development tools.
# They identify the APPLICATION, not the account: new credentials on the same
# phone are still the same account, and running two clients against it risks
# the session v1 depends on. Only a different phone number gives real headroom.
V2_TELEGRAM_API_ID=
V2_TELEGRAM_API_HASH=
# ingest/setup_session.py only (the one-time login).
V2_TELEGRAM_PHONE=
# Comma-separated channel usernames. THE channel list: the poller's roster lives
# here and nowhere in the tree, so its count is `grep ^V2_TELEGRAM_CHANNELS= .env
# | tr ',' '\n' | wc -l` on main-server.
V2_TELEGRAM_CHANNELS=
# Seconds between polling cycles (default 30).
V2_POLL_INTERVAL=30

# NASA FIRMS map key (free, issued at once: firms.modaps.eosdis.nasa.gov/api/
# map_key/). Unset, ingest/sources/fires.py prints a message and exits 0, so
# ingest-external reports success with no fires collected.
FIRMS_MAP_KEY=

# Comma-separated rclone destinations for nightly backups (ops/backup.py,
# ops/restore_test.py).
BACKUP_REMOTE=gdrive:palestine-v2-backups

# ── Operational alarms (ops/notify.py) ───────────────────────────────────────
# ntfy is the channel that rings (docs: ops/systemd/README.md "Alarm delivery").
# Defaults: http://127.0.0.1:8688, topic fawwaz-alerts, token read from
# /home/zaid/lifeos/state.json (`ntfy_token`). A restored box that does not run
# that ntfy must set these or NTFY_URL=off, or every alarm reaches only
# ops/alerts.ndjson.
NTFY_URL=
NTFY_TOPIC=
NTFY_TOKEN=
NTFY_TOKEN_FILE=
# Telegram fallback: a BotFather BOT, deliberately NOT the agent2 account:
# alarms over agent2 are how the scarcest asset in this project gets
# rate-limited, and a channel that dies under load is worse than none because
# its silence reads as good news. A bot is a separate identity with its own
# token over the plain HTTPS Bot API — no Telethon, no session file, nothing
# shared with the poller.
#   1. TOKEN: @BotFather -> /mytoken -> pick the bot (reusing @abed_hermes_bot
#      is fine; any number of senders may share one bot)
#   2. CHAT ID: your own numeric id — ask @userinfobot.
#      Do NOT use /getUpdates to find it: Telegram allows exactly ONE
#      getUpdates consumer per bot, and the Hermes gateway long-polls
#      @abed_hermes_bot continuously — polling it here would break a working
#      assistant to configure an alarm. sendMessage has no such limit.
# Unset is a normal state: alarms still land in ops/alerts.ndjson.
ALERT_BOT_TOKEN=
ALERT_CHAT_ID=

# ── read from the PROCESS ENVIRONMENT only (a line here does nothing) ────────
# Shown with the default the code uses when unset.
#
# The usage ledger's IP salt (serve/mcp_usage.py). Unset, a random salt is drawn
# at every API start, so one caller counts as two across a restart. Set it in
# the API unit's drop-in to keep callers stable.
#MCP_USAGE_SALT=
# The API the MCP tools call (serve/mcp_server.py). A dev API on :7871 needs it.
#PALESTINE_API=http://127.0.0.1:7870
# OAuth issuer and state file (serve/mcp_oauth.py).
#PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://live-api.zaidlab.xyz
#OAUTH_STATE_PATH=<checkout>/.keys/oauth-state.json
# Backups (ops/backup.py): local staging directory and the passphrase file.
#BACKUP_DIR=/opt/backups/palestine-v2
#BACKUP_KEYFILE=~/.config/palestine-v2/backup.key
# v1, read-only. PALESTINE_V1_ROOT is read by ops/evidence.py alone (set it to
# /nonexistent to prove the as_of harnesses survive v1's absence); every other
# v1 read hard-codes /opt/stacks/palestine (PLAN §7 P1-C.7 puts them behind one
# setting).
#PALESTINE_V1_ROOT=/opt/stacks/palestine
# v1's MiniMax alarm (ops/minimax_alarm.py); MINIMAX_CHECK=0 leaves it off the
# watchdog on a box where v1 does not run.
#V1_ALERTS_CONTAINER=params-alerts-api
#V1_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8081
#V1_CHECKPOINT_DB=/opt/stacks/palestine/services/westbank-alerts/data/checkpoints.db
#MINIMAX_CHECK=1
# The PC's llama-swap, probed by the analyst's backpressure (analyst/backpressure.py).
#LLAMA_SWAP_URL=http://100.114.104.95:8480
# The weekly maintenance run's model (ops/maintain.sh).
#MAINTAIN_MODEL=claude-opus-5
